22:46:38 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: In online first‑person shooters, the game isn’t just tracking a bullet on one machine—it has to faithfully share that bullet’s path with all other players, despite network delays. How this works depends on whether the weapon is **hitscan** (instant) or **projectile‑based** (a physical object with travel time). Both approaches use a mix of client prediction, lag compensation, and server authority to make the experience feel responsive and fair. ### 1. Hitscan (Instant bullets) For weapons like sniper rifles or assault rifles that fire “instant” beams, there is no real trajectory simulation over time. When a player pulls the trigger: - The client traces a ray (or a tiny cylinder) from the player’s camera into the 3D world, looking for the first hit. - The client sends a **fire event** to the server, including the precise timestamp and aim direction. - The server doesn’t trust the client’s hit result (to prevent cheating). Instead, it re‑creates the shot using **lag compensation**: - It “rewinds” all other players to where they were at the moment the shooter pulled the trigger (using their stored position history). - Then it performs its own ray trace against those past positions. If the server’s trace hits a player, it’s a registered hit. - The server tells all clients the outcome; the shooter’s client gets instant visual feedback from its own prediction, and the target receives the damage update. This is simple and feels instantaneous, but it doesn’t model bullet travel, drop, or penetration over time. ### 2. Projectile bullets (physics‑based trajectories) When bullets have actual flight time—like rockets, grenades, or “realistic” rifles with bullet drop—the game spawns a **networked physics object**. Real‑time tracking involves several layers: #### Spawning and simulation - The shooter’s client predicts: immediately after firing, the client spawns a local visual projectile that follows a deterministic path (initial velocity