my. Suppose you fire a slow rocket. By the time the rocket reaches where the enemy *was*, the enemy has moved. Instead of relying on the server’s current view, many games check hits against the enemy’s historical hitboxes **at the specific server tick** the projectile occupies that area. This is sometimes called “backward recreation” and makes dodging feel natural, because it respects what the shooter saw on their screen. #### Optimisation and fairness - **Client authority vs. server authority**: Most competitive games use server‑side hit registration, even for projectiles, to stop cheats. The client may show sparks/blood early, but only a server confirmation deals damage. - **Network tick rate**: A higher server tick rate (128 Hz vs 30 Hz) means smaller simulation steps and more accurate projectile paths, reducing “what you see isn’t what you get” moments. - **Bandwidth**: Sending full trajectory data for every bullet would flood the network. Usually the server only sends spawn info and final impact; clients predict the rest using the same physics rules. ### Why does it feel instant? The combination of client prediction and lag compensation hides most of the latency. You see your own weapon effects immediately; the server’s authoritative check happens in the background, and the results are rolled back on your screen only if there’s a mismatch (which is rare when hitboxes align). So, whether it’s a hitscan instant beam or a slow‑flying cannonball, the game tracks bullet interactions by running a **synchronised simulation across the network**, with the server always making the final decision—but clever enough to look back in time so that what you aimed at actually gets hit.
